+The genuinely new avenue I propose is the `danger-clearing coefficient
+character` test. The full tree allows a dangerous zero part to be neutralized
+only by a typed nonzero sibling choice. In the first live D=108 group, the two
+rows share the top `(10,25,6,4)` node but differ in whether the next selected
+child is zero or nonzero. The coefficient compiler should not merely solve
+each row; it should extract the character/residue contribution of a nonzero
+orbit that clears danger and test whether that contribution can be compatible
+with the child A.3 ODE. If the character is forced nonzero at the parent and
+forced zero at the child, the first live group dies by a new structural rule.
