+## 7. Strongest proof attack
+
+The strongest proof attack is not a bigger census. It is a local-to-global
+coefficient non-realisability theorem:
+
+For every promoted full-major-tree survivor, the node-local A.3 sibling
+coefficient system either has no solution, or any solution descends to a
+theorem-(T) monomial-Jacobian system that is impossible.
+
+The first lemma to try is narrower and more promising:
+
+`Danger-clearing lemma.` Suppose a full-tree survivor contains a zero sibling
+part at a dangerous parent and the selected route clears danger only by choosing
+a nonzero orbit. Then the cyclic quotient A.3 ODE forces a nontrivial
+parent character/residue that cannot glue to a later selected zero child.
+
+Why this is the right attack:
+
+- It explains the exact difference between the dead D=105/D=117 rows and the
+  first live D=108 rows. The dead rows fail by forced zero major sibling while
+  danger remains. The live D=108 group survives by interleaving zero and nonzero
+  sibling choices.
+- It attacks the local structure before global interpolation creates artificial
+  witness columns.
+- It is testable on two adjacent nodes, not on a 100-degree global polynomial.
+- If it succeeds, it may promote from a D=108 kill to an all-degree rule because
+  the pattern is typed by `(P,Q,A,b)` and the danger automaton, not by degree.
+
+The proof skeleton I would hand to a lane:
+
+1. Normalize each node with the variable/ring map declared: coefficient field,
+   `pi`, quotient variable `z=pi^A`, zero part `pi^b`, and nonzero orbit
+   factors.
+2. Write Moh A.3 as a cyclic quotient ODE with all leader vanishings branched.
+3. Extract the invariant term that changes when the selected child is a
+   nonzero orbit rather than the zero factor.
+4. Prove that this invariant is functorial under the child map used by the
+   full major tree.
+5. Apply it to the shared D=108 top node. If row A and row B demand incompatible
+   child invariants, kill the group. If not, keep the invariant as a necessary
+   coefficient decoration for the moment engine.
+
+This proof attack respects the fallacy guardrails: it separates flag/place/
+series, it treats `REPRESENTATIVE` as a floor only, it does not use pole
+identities without vertex hypotheses, it normal-forms in the declared quotient,
+and it never turns a lower bound into attainment.
+
+## 8. Strongest counterexample attack
+
+The strongest counterexample attack is to stop searching broad families and
+construct the first D=108 survivor as far as the promoted constraints allow.
+
+Target:
+
+`(n,m,D)=(108,72,36), M=(24,78,106), V4=5`, rows A and B from Section 1.
+
+Counterexample pipeline:
+
+1. Solve or partially solve the parent sibling coefficient system
+   `(P,Q,A,b)=(10,25,6,4)`.
+2. Try both child continuations `(3,7,2,1)` and `(12,28,3,0)`.
+3. If either row has an exact sibling witness, emit route-decorated branch data:
+   zero/nonzero decisions, orbit multiplicities, constants, and bottom
+   variables.
+4. Feed only that legal decoration to the tree-decorated moment map with
+   nonproper columns typed.
+5. If global moments survive, run theorem-(T) descent and collision ideals.
+6. Only then try formal-germ algebraization.
+
+This is adversarial in the right way. It attacks the first survivor after two
+degree fronts have been killed. It uses the promoted tree, not the old path
+screen. It gives exact intermediate artifacts that proof lanes can turn around
+and try to refute. If it survives all local and moment checks, the campaign has
+a genuinely live formal counterexample seed rather than a census ghost.
+
+## 9. Decisive experiment / software acceleration
+
+Build `d108_sibling_coeff_compile.py` as a desk-scale compiler around cyclic
+quotient normal forms.
+
+Minimum interface:
+
+- Input: `(P,Q,A,b)`, zero multiplicity, nonzero orbit multiplicities, selected
+  child mode, and leading constants.
+- Ring declaration: coefficient field, `pi`, quotient `z=pi^A`, orbit factor
+  variables, generator order, and saturation variables.
+- Output: one of `UNSAT`, `SAT_WITNESS`, `RANK_DEFECT`, or `NEEDS_BRANCH`,
+  with controls and exact ring maps.
+
+First batch:
+
+1. Parent D=108 node `(10,25,6,4)`.
+2. Row-A child `(3,7,2,1)`.
+3. Row-B child `(12,28,3,0)`.
+4. Appendix-II positive/negative controls.
+
+Acceleration principle:
+
+Do quotient and leader-branching before Groebner. A naive polynomial system in
+`pi` will carry fake symmetry and fake rank. A quotient system in `z` should
+make the parent and child systems small enough for the required `<10 min,
+<4 GB` budget. If a system exceeds that budget, the lane should emit modular
+rank and the exact unsimplified ideal rather than escalating silently.
+
+Decisive outcomes:
+
+- `UNSAT` for parent: the first D=108 group dies and the proof lane extracts
+  the local obstruction.
+- parent `SAT`, both children `UNSAT`: D=108 dies by child gluing.
+- parent and one child `SAT`: tree-decorated moment becomes mandatory.
+- inconsistent modular ranks across primes: ring map or saturation error; do
+  not promote.
